nding school  only remotely do you feel the kids that  are learning virtually  their mental health is declining 100  percent sherry glassman is a school  psychologist in rocco's district where  there's a hybrid program  some students in person and others are  virtual  our virtual students are dealing with a  lot of isolation  a lot of screen burnout glassman credits  rocco's improvement with early  intervention  proactive parents and returning to  school in person  how many calls do you receive a day from  parents worried about their children  at least five to ten calls a day and  it's all about mental health issues  dr maria urovi is a pediatrician in new  jersey  she said the calls she gets are about  kids as young as kindergarten  through college what are the biggest  signs you should look for if you think  your child is  really struggling not to want to be  involved in activities not wanting to  eat just staying in the room  sleeping in a september survey more than  half of children  ages 11 to 17 reported they had thoughts  of suicide or self-harm  nearly half or every day in the previous  two weeks  they were also more likely than any  other age group to have moderate to  severe  symptoms of anxiety and depression what  should you do if you think your child is  going to harm themselves straight to the  hospital  and what should you do if you think your  child is depressed get help  get absolute health call yeah call a  psychologist call somebody get help for  them and for the family rocco told us  speaking publicly to  us about his mental health has also  helped  that definitely made me feel happy and  like a lot better that  i was making an impact on other people  it's not easy to talk about your mental  health do you realize that that was  a really brave thing you did yeah  why is it so important to keep this  conversation going  because it definitely normalizes these  feelings and it ends that stigma  of mental health issues i mean there  should be no stigma around it  people need to talk about these things  and know that other people are going  through the same things they are  one clinician at a mental health care  facility here in new jersey told me they  are  overwhelmed by the number of calls from  parents of struggling kids  as we face this mental health crisis  rocco school psychologist says  she hopes bringing kids back in person  is prioritized  tony all right meg thank you very much  bravo to rocco for speaking up yeah  officially what i was going to do we  like to hear that remember we all  reacted and thought that was so brave  he does he i know he's not going to want  to hear this but he's a very  good-looking little kid  [Laughter]  very good-looking little kid and so i  praise him and his mom for allowing them  to come  and talk so candidly yeah and i like the  fact that he's owning it saying yeah  he's glad that he did it because it will  help other kids  yeah it does get better it does get  better all right meg thank you very much
 

